# 01. Purpose

This document defines the infrastructure architecture required to develop, test, deploy, operate, scale, monitor, recover, and maintain AEGIS.

AEGIS is designed as a high-stakes healthcare and biosecurity decision-support platform.

Infrastructure therefore cannot be treated as an afterthought.

The infrastructure must preserve:

```text
Availability
Data integrity
Security
Traceability
Observability
Reproducibility
Recoverability
Scalability
Controlled change
```

The infrastructure architecture must support the complete AEGIS lifecycle:

```text
Developer
   ↓
Local
   ↓
Development
   ↓
Test
   ↓
Staging
   ↓
Production
   ↓
Monitoring
   ↓
Incident Response
   ↓
Recovery
   ↓
Continuous Improvement
```

---

# 02. Infrastructure Principles

AEGIS infrastructure follows these principles.

## 02.1 Infrastructure must serve the architecture

Infrastructure must implement the requirements established by Documents 01–14.

It must not create a second architecture.

---

## 02.2 Production must be reproducible

A production environment should be reproducible from:

```text
Source Code
+
Configuration
+
Infrastructure Definition
+
Database Migration
+
Container Images
+
Secrets
+
Deployment Configuration
```

---

## 02.3 Environment separation

AEGIS must not treat development as a miniature production environment with uncontrolled differences.

The environments must be intentionally separated while maintaining architectural parity.

---

## 02.4 Secure by default

Infrastructure should assume:

```text
Every network boundary matters.
Every credential matters.
Every service boundary matters.
Every deployment is potentially risky.
```

---

## 02.5 Human-controlled consequential operations

Infrastructure must never create an accidental path where an AI system can independently bypass:

```text
authentication
authorization
audit
human approval
```

---

## 02.6 Observable by design

Infrastructure components must expose sufficient telemetry to determine:

```text
What happened?
When?
Where?
Why?
Which component?
Which version?
Which request?
Which user/session where appropriate?
```

---

