# 05. Deployment Model

AEGIS should use a containerized deployment model.

The primary deployment unit is a container image.

Conceptually:

```text
Source
 ↓
Build
 ↓
Test
 ↓
Container Image
 ↓
Registry
 ↓
Deployment
 ↓
Environment
```

---

# 06. Docker Architecture

Every deployable AEGIS service should have a reproducible container definition.

At minimum:

```text
Frontend
Backend/API
Worker/Processing Services
Supporting Services
```

where those services actually exist in the approved application architecture.

---

# 07. Container Requirements

Production containers should:

```text
run as non-root where practical
use pinned dependencies
have predictable startup behavior
have health checks
avoid embedded secrets
produce structured logs
handle graceful shutdown
```

---

# 08. Container Immutability

A running production container should not be manually modified.

The correct process is:

```text
Code Change
 ↓
Build New Image
 ↓
Test
 ↓
Deploy New Version
```

not:

```text
SSH into container
 ↓
Modify files
 ↓
Hope nothing breaks
```

---

# 09. Image Versioning

Container images must be identifiable.

Example:

```text
aegis-api:<version>
```

The exact tagging convention should eventually include a traceable release identifier.

Avoid relying exclusively on:

```text
latest
```

for production deployments.

---

# 10. Software Supply Chain

Production builds should be traceable to:

```text
Git commit
Dependency versions
Build process
Container image
Release version
Deployment
```

This allows AEGIS to answer:

> Which exact software produced this result?

---

# 11. Environment Architecture

AEGIS uses five logical environments:

```text
LOCAL
DEVELOPMENT
TEST
STAGING
PRODUCTION
```

Each environment has a different purpose.

---

# 12. LOCAL

Purpose:

```text
rapid development
experimentation
component development
debugging
```

Typical characteristics:

```text
Docker
local PostgreSQL
local Redis Streams
local FastAPI
local frontend
synthetic data
```

Local infrastructure must never depend on production credentials.

---

# 13. DEVELOPMENT

Purpose:

```text
team integration
feature integration
service integration
early workflow testing
```

Development should approximate the production architecture without requiring production scale.

---

# 14. TEST

Purpose:

```text
automated tests
integration tests
contract tests
security tests
E2E tests
regression tests
```

Test infrastructure must be isolated from development data.

---

# 15. STAGING

Purpose:

```text
production-like validation
release candidate testing
operational journey testing
stakeholder validation
deployment verification
```

Staging should be as architecturally similar to production as practical.

---

# 16. PRODUCTION

Production is the authoritative operational environment.

Production must have:

```text
strict access control
protected secrets
persistent storage
backup strategy
monitoring
audit
alerting
recovery procedures
controlled deployment
```

---

# 17. Environment Isolation

The following must never be casually shared between environments:

```text
production database
production credentials
production secrets
production access tokens
production storage
```

---

# 18. Configuration Architecture

Configuration must be externalized.

Conceptually:

```text
Application Code
       +
Environment Configuration
       +
Secrets
       ↓
Runtime
```

Do not hard-code environment-specific configuration into application code.

---

# 19. Configuration Categories

Separate:

```text
Application Configuration
Infrastructure Configuration
Environment Configuration
Security Secrets
Feature Configuration
External Integration Configuration
```

---

# 20. Secrets

Secrets include:

```text
database credentials
API keys
authentication secrets
encryption keys
external service credentials
AI provider credentials
```

Secrets must not be committed to Git.

They must be injected securely at runtime.

---

# 21. Secret Lifecycle

Secrets should support:

```text
creation
storage
access control
rotation
revocation
replacement
audit
```

---

# 22. Database Architecture

PostgreSQL is the primary AEGIS relational data store.

It is responsible for durable system state.

Conceptually:

```text
FastAPI
   ↓
Domain / Service Layer
   ↓
PostgreSQL
```

---

# 23. PostgreSQL Responsibilities

PostgreSQL should hold authoritative structured state such as:

```text
users
roles
permissions
facilities
entities
events
alerts
investigations
assessments
recommendations
decisions
audit records
configuration
```

The exact tables and relationships remain governed by Document 06.

---

# 24. Database Ownership

The application architecture must define which service owns which data.

Services should not casually modify another service's internal tables.

Preferred pattern:

```text
Service
 ↓
Owns domain data
 ↓
Exposes contract
```

rather than:

```text
Service A
 ↓
directly edits
 ↓
Service B's tables
```

---

# 25. Database Migrations

Database changes must be version-controlled.

Conceptually:

```text
Migration 001
Migration 002
Migration 003
...
```

Deployment must know which database schema version is active.

---

# 26. Migration Safety

Before production migration:

```text
backup
 ↓
migration validation
 ↓
staging test
 ↓
production migration
 ↓
verification
```

Destructive schema changes require special planning.

---

# 27. Database Backups

Production PostgreSQL must have a defined backup strategy.

The strategy should address:

```text
backup frequency
retention
encryption
storage isolation
restore procedure
restore testing
```

---

# 28. Backup Principle

A backup that has never been restored successfully is not sufficient evidence of recoverability.

Therefore:

```text
Backup
 ↓
Restore Test
 ↓
Validation
```

must be part of the operational lifecycle.

---

# 29. Redis Streams

Redis Streams is the MVP event/streaming mechanism.

It should support asynchronous event processing where required by the architecture.

Conceptually:

```text
Producer
 ↓
Redis Stream
 ↓
Consumer
 ↓
Processing
 ↓
Persistent State
```

---

# 30. Redis Responsibilities

Redis should not silently become the authoritative permanent store for data that belongs in PostgreSQL.

Use Redis Streams for appropriate:

```text
events
queues
stream processing
temporary coordination
```

while PostgreSQL remains the primary durable relational source of truth.

---

# 31. Redis Failure

The system must define behavior when Redis becomes unavailable.

Potential behavior:

```text
stream processing pauses
 ↓
events remain protected according to architecture
 ↓
service recovers
 ↓
processing resumes
```

The exact recovery semantics must be implemented and tested.

---

# 32. Redis Consumer Architecture

Consumers must support:

```text
acknowledgement
retry
failure handling
dead-letter strategy where required
idempotency
observability
```

---

# 33. Duplicate Event Handling

AEGIS must assume that event delivery may sometimes be repeated.

Consumers should therefore use idempotent processing wherever the event architecture requires it.

---

# 34. Kafka Migration Readiness

The MVP uses Redis Streams.

The architecture must remain capable of eventually migrating high-volume or more complex event streaming to Kafka.

The application should therefore avoid deeply coupling business logic to Redis-specific semantics.

Prefer:

```text
Domain Event Contract
       ↓
Event Adapter
       ↓
Redis Streams
```

rather than embedding Redis behavior throughout business logic.

---

# 35. Future Kafka Migration

The desired migration path is:

```text
Application
     ↓
Event Contract
     ↓
Streaming Abstraction
     ↓
Redis Streams
```

Later:

```text
Application
     ↓
Same Event Contract
     ↓
Streaming Abstraction
     ↓
Kafka
```

The goal is to minimize business-domain changes during migration.

---

# 36. Object Storage

AEGIS may require durable storage for objects such as:

```text
documents
reports
evidence files
exports
artifacts
```

Only requirements explicitly approved by the product and data architecture should be implemented.

Object storage must not become an uncontrolled second database.

---

# 37. Storage Security

Stored objects should have:

```text
access control
encryption where required
retention rules
auditability
```

---

# 38. Frontend Deployment

The frontend should be deployed independently from backend services where practical.

Conceptually:

```text
User
 ↓
Frontend
 ↓
API
```

The frontend must not directly bypass the API security boundary to access protected backend resources.

---

# 39. Backend Deployment

FastAPI services should run as containerized workloads.

Conceptually:

```text
Client
 ↓
API Boundary
 ↓
FastAPI
 ↓
Domain Services
 ↓
PostgreSQL / Redis / external systems
```

---

# 40. Worker Architecture

Where asynchronous processing is required:

```text
FastAPI
 ↓
Redis Stream
 ↓
Worker
 ↓
Processing
 ↓
PostgreSQL
```

Workers must have:

```text
health checks
retry behavior
failure handling
structured logs
metrics
```

---

# 41. Health Checks

Every production service should expose meaningful health information.

Separate:

```text
Liveness
Readiness
Dependency Health
```

---

# 42. Liveness

Liveness answers:

> Is the process alive?

A liveness failure should trigger appropriate orchestration behavior.

---

# 43. Readiness

Readiness answers:

> Can this instance safely receive traffic?

A service may be alive while not being ready.

---

# 44. Dependency Health

Dependency health should help determine:

```text
database available?
Redis available?
external service available?
AI service available?
```

without falsely claiming that the entire application is healthy when a critical dependency is unavailable.

---

# 45. Networking

The production architecture should separate:

```text
Public Access
Application Network
Data Network
Management Access
```

where applicable.

Databases should not be unnecessarily exposed to the public internet.

---

# 46. API Boundary

External users should reach AEGIS through a controlled application/API boundary.

The architecture should enforce:

```text
authentication
authorization
rate controls where appropriate
TLS
request validation
logging
```

---

# 47. TLS

Production communication involving sensitive AEGIS data should use encrypted transport.

The infrastructure must manage certificate lifecycle appropriately.

---

# 48. Kubernetes Readiness

AEGIS should be Kubernetes-ready without requiring Kubernetes complexity prematurely.

The architecture should support eventual deployment of:

```text
Frontend
API
Workers
Supporting Services
```

as orchestrated workloads.

---

# 49. MVP Orchestration Principle

Do not introduce Kubernetes merely because the architecture is Kubernetes-ready.

The MVP should use the simplest infrastructure that can:

```text
run reliably
be reproduced
be tested
be secured
be monitored
```

while preserving the architectural boundaries required for future orchestration.

---

# 50. Kubernetes Migration Path

Conceptually:

```text
Docker
 ↓
Containerized Services
 ↓
Deployment Definitions
 ↓
Kubernetes
```

The application should not require a complete rewrite when orchestration changes.

---

# 51. Scaling Model

AEGIS should scale according to actual bottlenecks.

Potential scaling dimensions include:

```text
API instances
worker instances
database capacity
stream consumers
AI workloads
storage
```

---

# 52. Horizontal Scaling

Stateless services should be designed to support multiple instances.

Example:

```text
             ┌── API 1
Load Balancer┼── API 2
             └── API 3
```

assuming the application's state management supports this.

---

# 53. Stateful Scaling

PostgreSQL and Redis require different scaling strategies from stateless application services.

Do not treat:

```text
API
Database
Stream Infrastructure
```

as interchangeable workloads.

---

# 54. Autoscaling

Autoscaling should be introduced only where useful.

Possible signals include:

```text
CPU
memory
request rate
latency
queue depth
stream backlog
```

The exact thresholds must come from measured workload characteristics.

---

# 55. AI Infrastructure

AI must remain behind an explicit service boundary.

Conceptually:

```text
AEGIS Application
       ↓
AI Intelligence Service
       ↓
Model / Provider
```

The frontend should not directly control model infrastructure.

---

# 56. AI Infrastructure Principles

AI infrastructure must support:

```text
model versioning
configuration versioning
request tracing
output validation
failure handling
timeout handling
cost monitoring where applicable
```

---

# 57. AI Availability Failure

If AI becomes unavailable, AEGIS must not silently fabricate intelligence.

The system should transition to an explicit state such as:

```text
AI_UNAVAILABLE
```

or another approved degraded state.

---

# 58. AI Infrastructure Security

AI credentials must be isolated from frontend code.

The architecture should prevent users from extracting provider credentials through browser inspection or API responses.

---

# 59. External Services

External integrations must be treated as unreliable dependencies.

Every integration should define:

```text
timeout
retry
failure state
authentication
rate behavior
observability
```

---

# 60. Retry Policy

Retries must be controlled.

Blind retries can create:

```text
duplicate operations
traffic amplification
event duplication
cascading failure
```

Retry only operations that are safe to retry.

---

# 61. Circuit Breaking

Where external dependency failure could cascade into AEGIS, appropriate circuit-breaking or equivalent failure isolation should be considered.

---

# 62. Timeout Architecture

Every network-dependent operation should have an intentional timeout.

Never allow an external dependency to hang indefinitely.

---

