---

# 67. Production Deployment

Production deployments should be controlled.

At minimum:

```text
approved release
known artifact
known configuration
migration plan
rollback plan
post-deployment validation
```

---

# 68. Deployment Strategies

AEGIS should support controlled deployment strategies as operational maturity increases.

Potential patterns include:

```text
rolling deployment
blue/green deployment
canary deployment
```

The selected method should depend on infrastructure maturity and actual availability requirements.

---

# 69. MVP Deployment Strategy

For the first implementation, prioritize:

```text
simplicity
reproducibility
rollback capability
observability
```

over unnecessary deployment complexity.

---

# 70. Rollback

Every production release must have a rollback strategy.

Conceptually:

```text
Version N
 ↓
Version N+1
 ↓
Failure
 ↓
Rollback
 ↓
Version N
 ↓
Validation
```

---

# 71. Database Rollback

Application rollback and database rollback are not automatically equivalent.

Database migrations must be designed carefully.

Prefer migration strategies that allow safe application rollback where practical.

---

# 72. Zero-Downtime Consideration

If production availability requirements eventually require zero or near-zero downtime:

```text
backward-compatible changes
rolling deployment
health checks
connection draining
```

must be incorporated.

---

# 73. Infrastructure as Code

Infrastructure should eventually be represented as code.

Conceptually:

```text
Infrastructure Definition
 ↓
Version Control
 ↓
Review
 ↓
Validation
 ↓
Deployment
```

Manual infrastructure changes should be minimized.

---

# 74. Infrastructure Change Control

Infrastructure changes must follow the same discipline as application changes:

```text
change
 ↓
review
 ↓
test
 ↓
deploy
 ↓
observe
```

---

# 75. Production Access

Production access must be restricted.

Access should be:

```text
role-based
auditable
minimal
time-appropriate
```

where technically feasible.

---

# 76. Administrative Access

Administrative access should not rely on shared credentials.

Individual identity should be maintained where possible.

---

# 77. Production Database Access

Developers should not casually connect directly to the production database.

Normal application operations should go through controlled service interfaces.

---

# 78. Auditability

Infrastructure events should be traceable where appropriate:

```text
deployment
configuration change
database migration
access
security event
failure
rollback
```

---

# 79. Observability Integration

Document 12 defines AEGIS observability.

Infrastructure must feed that architecture.

The infrastructure layer should expose:

```text
application logs
infrastructure logs
metrics
health status
deployment events
resource utilization
dependency status
```

---

# 80. Structured Logging

Logs should be machine-readable where practical.

Important contextual fields may include:

```text
timestamp
service
environment
version
request ID
trace ID
event type
severity
```

Sensitive data must not be logged unnecessarily.

---

# 81. Correlation

A request should be traceable across relevant services.

Conceptually:

```text
User Action
 ↓
API Request
 ↓
Service
 ↓
Stream Event
 ↓
Worker
 ↓
Database
```

The system should preserve enough correlation information to investigate the workflow.

---

# 82. Infrastructure Metrics

Monitor meaningful infrastructure signals:

```text
CPU
memory
storage
network
request volume
latency
error rate
database connections
stream backlog
worker health
```

---

# 83. Alerting

Infrastructure alerts should distinguish:

```text
warning
critical
informational
```

Avoid creating alert noise that causes operators to ignore important signals.

---

# 84. Alert Fatigue

AEGIS infrastructure itself must not become an alert-generation problem.

Alerts should have:

```text
clear condition
severity
owner
expected action
```

where operationally relevant.

---

# 85. Data Retention

Retention policies must be defined according to:

```text
data type
purpose
regulatory requirements
security requirements
storage constraints
```

Do not invent retention periods without stakeholder, legal, regulatory, or organizational requirements.

---

# 86. Disaster Recovery

Disaster recovery must address:

```text
application failure
database failure
storage failure
infrastructure failure
deployment failure
credential compromise
regional infrastructure failure
```

where relevant to the final deployment environment.

---

# 87. Recovery Objectives

AEGIS should eventually define:

```text
RPO — Recovery Point Objective
RTO — Recovery Time Objective
```

These must be stakeholder-approved.

Do not invent numerical targets before requirements exist.

---

# 88. Disaster Recovery Flow

Conceptually:

```text
Failure
 ↓
Detection
 ↓
Incident Declaration
 ↓
Containment
 ↓
Recovery
 ↓
Validation
 ↓
Service Restoration
 ↓
Post-Incident Review
```

---

# 89. Backup Strategy

Backups should be:

```text
automated
protected
encrypted where required
retained
tested
recoverable
```

---

# 90. Recovery Testing

Recovery tests must eventually verify:

```text
database restoration
application restoration
configuration restoration
storage restoration
service restoration
```

---

# 91. Failure Domains

Infrastructure design should recognize different failure domains:

```text
application
container
host
database
network
availability zone
region
external provider
```

The actual supported failure domains depend on the final infrastructure provider.

---

# 92. Dependency Mapping

Each critical service should document:

```text
depends on
used by
failure impact
recovery behavior
```

This should correspond to the service architecture established earlier.

---

# 93. Infrastructure Dependency Graph

Conceptually:

```text
Frontend
   ↓
API
   ↓
Domain Services
 ┌─┴───────────┐
 ↓             ↓
PostgreSQL   Redis
               ↓
             Workers
               ↓
         Intelligence
```

The exact graph must follow the implementation architecture rather than being invented independently.

---

# 94. Capacity Planning

Before production scaling decisions:

```text
measure
 ↓
establish baseline
 ↓
identify bottleneck
 ↓
scale
 ↓
measure again
```

Do not prematurely optimize infrastructure based on theoretical traffic.

---

# 95. Cost Awareness

Infrastructure should be designed with operational cost visibility.

Track major cost drivers where applicable:

```text
compute
database
storage
network
AI inference
observability
external services
```

---

# 96. Resource Limits

Services should eventually have explicit resource expectations.

For containerized workloads:

```text
CPU
memory
storage
```

should be defined based on measurements.

---

# 97. Resource Exhaustion

The system must be tested for:

```text
memory exhaustion
disk exhaustion
connection exhaustion
stream backlog
database saturation
```

and must produce observable failure behavior.

---

# 98. Graceful Shutdown

Services must handle termination correctly.

Conceptually:

```text
Shutdown Signal
 ↓
Stop accepting new work
 ↓
Finish safe work
 ↓
Close connections
 ↓
Exit
```

This is particularly important for workers and event consumers.

---

# 99. Deployment Safety

Never deploy blindly into production.

Required chain:

```text
Build
 ↓
Test
 ↓
Review
 ↓
Stage
 ↓
Validate
 ↓
Deploy
 ↓
Observe
```

---

# 100. Environment Parity

The environments should share:

```text
same architecture
same service boundaries
same contracts
same container behavior
```

while differing appropriately in:

```text
scale
credentials
data
external endpoints
security boundaries
```

---

# 101. Local-to-Production Principle

The developer should be able to understand:

```text
what runs locally
what runs in CI
what runs in staging
what runs in production
```

without discovering major architectural differences only during deployment.

---

# 102. Development Bootstrap

A new developer should eventually be able to:

```text
clone repository
 ↓
install prerequisites
 ↓
configure local environment
 ↓
start containers
 ↓
run migrations
 ↓
seed synthetic data
 ↓
start application
 ↓
run tests
```

with documented commands.

---

# 103. Reproducible Development

The local environment should minimize:

```text
"works on my machine"
```

problems.

Docker should be used to make core infrastructure reproducible.

---

# 104. Seed Data

Development and testing should have controlled synthetic datasets.

Example categories:

```text
normal operations
alerts
investigations
assessment states
response states
failure scenarios
```

These must remain synthetic unless approved data is explicitly introduced.

---

# 105. Production Data Protection

Production data must not automatically flow into:

```text
developer machines
AI development environments
test environments
local databases
```

---

# 106. Data Promotion

The normal direction should be:

```text
Synthetic Data
 ↓
Development
 ↓
Test
 ↓
Staging
```

not:

```text
Production
 ↓
Developer Laptop
```

---

# 107. Release Artifact

